[Event "French Defense: the wall and the counterpunch"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/rd263BaC"]

{ Today we learn the French Defense. A solid wall, and a counterpunch always loaded. } 1. e4 { He grabs the middle. Answer one step only: pawn to e6. That is the French. [%cal Ye7e6] } 1... e6 { The wall goes up. One quiet pawn, and the fight for the centre is only just starting. } (1... e5 { A fine move, and a whole other world. } 2. Nf3 { That is the open game. Today we build the French wall. }) (1... c5 { The Sicilian. Sharp and famous, but not this lesson. } 2. Nf3 { Another day, another fight. }) 2. d4 { He takes it all. Now hit back: d5, staking your claim. [%cal Yd7d5] } 2... d5 { Right back at him. Two pawns stand face to face, and the fight for the centre is on. } (2... c5 { A Sicilian idea. Here it lets his pawn charge past. } 3. d5 { He owns the middle. In the French, d5 belongs to you. }) (2... Nf6 { The knight runs into the pawns. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { He chases your knight and grabs the whole board. }) 3. e5 { He locks the middle. Your whole plan is one move: c5, biting the chain at its base. [%csl Rd4][%cal Yc7c5] } 3... c5 { The French counterpunch. His chain hangs from d4. Chew on it. [%cal Gc5d4] } (3... Bd7 { A quiet, useful move too — it develops and keeps c5 in hand. } 4. c3 c5 { And you strike anyway. Often the same house by another door. }) (3... Ne7 { Also fine. The knight often hops to f5 later. } 4. c3 c5 { But this punch comes first, sooner or later. }) (3... Nc6 { The knight blocks your own c pawn. The punch is gone. } 4. Nf3 { He builds in peace while you sit cramped. }) (3... f6 { The other lever, but far too soon. } 4. Bd3 { Your king side is airy and his bishop grins at it. c5 first. }) (3... b6 { Too slow: c5 is the whole point of this pawn chain, and this spends a move on the queen side instead. } 4. c3 { He gets a dream center for free. }) 4. c3 { He props the chain. Pile on: knight to c6 leans on d4. [%cal Yb8c6] } 4... Nc6 { Good. A second attacker lands on d4, and the knight finishes developing itself too. } (4... Qb6 { Good square, and we are heading there. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 { Same position. The knight and queen just swapped turns. }) (4... cxd4 { You may, but keep the tension a while. } 5. cxd4 { See it: now his other knight gets the c3 square for free. }) (4... Bd7 { A useful piece later. } 5. Nf3 { But bring the fighters first: knight and queen. }) 5. Nf3 { He guards d4 again. Now the queen: out to b6, a second fist on d4. [%cal Yd8b6] } 5... Qb6 { Queen and knight both glare at d4. He must babysit that pawn forever. [%cal Gb6d4,Gc6d4] } (5... Bd7 { Also a real line. } 6. Be2 Nge7 { Fine chess. Ours sends the queen to bully d4 first. }) (5... Nge7 { A fine move order too — f5 is the dream square either way. } 6. Na3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nf5 { The same song. Learn ours with the queen first. }) (5... Nf6 { Look again: the e5 pawn simply eats that knight. [%cal Re5f6] }) (5... cxd4 { Fine too: it just trades a move early, and you reach the same position either way. } 6. cxd4 { Play the queen to b6 next and we are back in our line. }) 6. Be2 { The modest bishop move. Trade pawns now and open the c column. [%cal Yc5d4] } 6... cxd4 { The trade is on. His base pawn is gone, and the c column is yours to use. } (6... Nh6 { Careful with the order. } 7. Bxh6 gxh6 { He may rip your king side. Trade pawns first, then hop the knight. }) (6... Bd7 { Fine and calm. } 7. O-O { But ask d4 the question before he settles in. }) (6... Nge7 { The knight went out one door too early. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 8. b4 { His pawns arrive with tempo on your queen. }) 7. cxd4 { Knight to h6 now, sneaking toward f5. And d4 is not free: his queen watches it. [%cal Yg8h6,Rd1d4] } 7... Nh6 { On its way. From h6 the knight reaches f5 next, eyeing d4 and e3 both. } (7... Nxd4 { The pawn was poisoned! } 8. Nxd4 { A knight gone for a pawn. If your queen grabs back, his queen takes her. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (7... Nge7 { Just as good, honestly. } 8. Na3 Nf5 { Same dream square by the front door. }) (7... Bd7 { A tempo slower. } 8. Nc3 { The knight wanted f5 right now. }) (7... Bb4+ { The check achieves nothing. } 8. Nc3 { He blocks and develops. Your bishop must soon move again. }) 8. Nc3 { He develops. Finish the trip: knight to f5. [%cal Yh6f5] } 8... Nf5 { Beautiful. Three pieces lean on d4 and you are fully equal. He does the worrying now. [%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4,Gb6d4] } (8... Bd7 { Too slow. The moment was now. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Your king side is torn and the f5 dream is dead. He is clearly better here. }) (8... Be7 { The bishop can wait. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Same tear. The knight had to jump first. }) 0-1


[Event "French Defense: the bishop that blocks the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/pvSngwg1"]
[FEN "r1b1kbnr/pp3ppp/1qn1p3/2ppP3/3P4/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 3 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1kbnr/pp3ppp/1qn1p3/2ppP3/3P4/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_3_6"]

{ Back to this locked, chain-versus-chain position from the French. Look sharp today: pretty is not always safe. } 6. Bd3 { The bishop stands tall on d3, blocking his own queen's view of d4. How do you punish that? [%csl Rd3] } 6... cxd4 { Yes. Open the position first. } (6... Nh6 { The usual route, but he has a saving trade now. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { The d4 target is gone. You stand fine; the fun is smaller. }) (6... Bd7 { Right piece, wrong beat. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Again the target vanishes. Trade pawns first and it cannot. }) (6... a6 { A useful wait, but he escapes. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Target gone again. }) (6... c4 { Tempting. But the tension on d4 was your treasure. } 7. Bc2 { He tucks the bishop away and stands better. The c column play is gone. }) 7. cxd4 { Count the guards of d4: one knight, nothing more. But his bishop dreams of a check on b5. Cover that square. [%cal Rd3b5] } 7... Bd7 { Perfect. No more b5 tricks, and now knight takes d4 is a true threat. } (7... Nxd4 { One move too soon! } 8. Nxd4 Qxd4 9. Bb5+ { The check uncovers the d column. } 9... Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qxd4 { And your queen is gone. Cover b5 first. }) (7... Nh6 { You are missing a gift! The bishop blocks the queen, so d4 hangs. } 8. Nc3 { He saves it. The chance has passed and he stands better. }) (7... Nge7 { The gift slips by. } 8. Nc3 { Now the grab fails: his knight would leap to b5, hitting queen and c7. }) 8. O-O { He castles, offering the d4 pawn for open lines. Take it — but with the right piece first. } 8... Nxd4 { The knight first. It can be traded off safely. } (8... Qxd4 { The wrong grabber! } 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 { Queen for a knight and a pawn. The knight had to take first. }) (8... Rc8 { You hesitated, and the moment to grab the pawn has already passed. } 9. Be2 { The bishop slinks home and d4 is guarded again. The moment was now. }) (8... Nge7 { Develop later. The pawn was ripe now. } 9. Nc3 { Guarded again, with b5 leaps in the air. }) (8... Be7 { The gift had a time limit. } 9. Be2 { Safe again — the pawn is guarded once more, and the moment has passed. }) 9. Nxd4 { He recaptures. Now yes, the queen may take: the d3 bishop blinds his own lady. [%csl Rd3] } 9... Qxd4 { A clean pawn in the bag. } 10. Nc3 { His last trick: the knight arrives with b5 dreams. One tiny pawn move slams that door forever. Which one? } 10... a6 { Exactly. No knight to b5, no bishop to b5, not ever. A pawn up: develop and enjoy it. [%csl Ga6] } (10... Qxe5 { Greed number two. Now every white piece wakes up furious. } 11. Re1 Qb8 12. Nxd5 { And this knight is poison: open the e column and his rook crashes in. One pawn was plenty. }) (10... Qb6 { Safe, and still a pawn up. } 11. a3 { See, he plays the little pawn move himself. a6 at once was cleaner. }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop leaps into a storm. } 11. Be3 { He hits back with tempo and you are suddenly the one in trouble. }) 0-1


[Event "French Defense: the a3 sneak"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
