{ Sometimes he ducks the fight and swaps in the middle. A mirror, but only one of you brought a plan. } 3. exd5 { He trades right away. Which piece keeps your pawn shape the cleanest? } 3... exd5 { Good. The pawn recaptures, your queen stays home, and the shape stays clean. } (3... Qxd5 { The queen strolls out way too early. } 4. Nf3 { He develops for free while she looks for a home. Pawn takes was the move. }) 4. Nf3 { A mirror now — he develops the same knight you are about to. What is the natural way to answer, piece for piece? } 4... Nf6 { Copied calmly. Development first, before anything sharp. } (4... Nc6 { Fine as well. } 5. Nc3 { Just mind the bishop pin on b5 in these mirrors. }) (4... Bd6 { Good square. We simply bring knights before bishops. } 5. c4 { And see how the early poke asks questions at d5. }) (4... Be7 { A shade shy. } 5. Bd3 { d6 is the fighting square for that bishop. }) (4... Bg4 { A real pin on his knight. But there is a sharp answer. } 5. h3 Be6 { He backs the bishop off rather than press on, and you are a shade more comfortable than in our main line. }) 5. Bd3 { His bishop comes out, eyeing your king side. Where does yours belong in this mirror? } 5... Bd6 { Good square. Your bishop stares right back at his king side too. } (5... c5 { The French break works here too. Completely sound. } 6. c3 { It opens the game at once. Our line keeps the mirror one more beat. }) (5... Bg4 { Right idea, one move early. } 6. O-O { Castle first, then pin in comfort. }) (5... Be7 { Too modest — from e7 the bishop only watches, while d6 lets it fight. } 6. h3 { On d6 the bishop fights; on e7 it watches. }) 6. O-O { He tucks his king away. What is the safest reply in a mirror like this? } 6... O-O { King safe before any fight starts. Mirrors stay balanced this way. } (6... Bg4 { The pin one beat too soon! } 7. Re1+ Be7 { The rook check drags your proud bishop back to block. Castle first. }) (6... Be6 { Solid enough, though it blocks your own c pawn for now. } 7. Nc3 { But the king was waiting his turn. }) (6... Nc6 { The knight takes c6 from your own pawn. } 7. Re1+ Be7 { And the e column check costs your bishop its post. }) 7. Bg5 { The first real question of the game: he pins your knight to the queen. Do you have the same trick? } 7... Bg4 { Mirrored. His knight is pinned to the queen too now, and he must be just as careful. } (7... Be6 { A calm choice too, and just as good. } 8. Re1 { A long quiet game. The g4 pin bites harder. }) (7... h6 { You may poke first. } 8. Bh4 { The bishop steps back and keeps pinning. No harm either way. }) (7... c6 { Sturdy, but the pin was free. } 8. Nbd2 { He improves for nothing. }) (7... Nbd7 { Playable, though his c4 poke gets annoying soon. } 8. c4 { Pin first, then this knight. }) 8. Nbd2 { He breaks the pin politely with the knight. One more piece wants to copy the mirror — which one, and where? } 8... Nbd7 { Done. Two bishops already aim at his king side, your last knight can swing to b6 or e5, and the position is dead level — exactly what this whole line was built to reach. } (8... c5 { Breaking the mirror at once, and objectively the most challenging try. } 9. h3 Bh5 { Keep the pin; a rich open game. Ours is the quieter road. }) (8... h6 { Fine — it just asks the bishop a question before anything else. } 9. Bh4 { Everything holds — the pin stays and nothing is lost by the pause. }) (8... Re8 { A good rook move too. } 9. h3 { Your other knight was itching to develop first, but no harm. }) 0-1


