{ Last door of this repertoire. Whatever he tries here, our answer is the simplest chess in the book. } 3. Nc3 { The knight guards e4, his most popular try. Which pawn grabs the loose one and trades the fuss away? } 3... dxe4 { Good. The pawn comes off, and the position simplifies right away. } (3... Nf6 { The Classical. Excellent and rich. } 4. Bg5 { The famous pin, and hours of homework. Our trade keeps the evening short. }) (3... Bb4 { The Winawer. Sharp, famous, double edged. } 4. e5 { Both kings run for their lives. Learn it someday; today we keep it clean. }) (3... Be7 { Too polite. The Winawer and Classical both fight harder than this. } 4. e5 { He grabs space for free. }) (3... Nc6 { The knight sits on your c pawn. No c5, no French. } 4. e5 { He clamps, and your counterplay never comes. }) 4. Nxe4 { He recaptures in the centre. One knight move now sets up the rest of this whole plan — can you see which? } 4... Nd7 { The key move. This knight exists for one job: taking back on f6. } (4... Nf6 { Natural, but watch your pawns. } 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 { Cracked. The d7 knight was born to take back here. }) (4... Bd7 { The fortress plan: bishop to c6 behind the wall. Solid and slow. } 5. Bd3 Bc6 { Playable. Ours frees the pieces faster. }) (4... Be7 { Quiet, but it skips the d7 knight's whole reason for being. } 5. Nf3 { The d7 knight plan is the smooth one. }) 5. Nf3 { He develops the other knight. Bring your last knight out too — where does it offer a trade on your own terms? } 5... Ngf6 { Right knight. This one offers the trade, and the d7 knight stays ready to take back. } (5... Ndf6 { The wrong horse! This one exists to take back later. } 6. Ng3 { He dodges, and your knights trip over each other. }) (5... Be7 { Fine, though it skips the trade this whole line is built around. } 6. Bd3 { The knight hop is more to the point. }) 6. Nxf6+ { He trades on your terms after all. Which knight was born to recapture here? } 6... Nxf6 { That is the whole point of d7 — a clean, active recapture. } (6... Qxf6 { The queen takes, and becomes the target. } 7. Bg5 { With tempo! She runs again while he develops for free. }) (6... gxf6 { Your king side cracks for nothing. } 7. g3 { He will aim at the holes forever. The knight was placed to take back. }) 7. Bd3 { Pieces come off, but the plan remains. Which pawn break have you played in almost every chapter now? } 7... c5 { The move you know by heart. His centre pawn is the target, same as always. } (7... Bd7 { Equally good: the bishop aims at c6. } 8. O-O { We strike with the pawn first, while his center is tall. }) (7... b6 { Freeing the French bishop. Lovely idea, one beat late. } 8. c3 { He cements d4 and your c5 gets harder. }) (7... Be7 { Castle first chess. } 8. Qe2 { Fine, but c5 is the moment. }) 8. dxc5 { He trades rather than defend the pawn. Which piece recaptures and comes out fighting at the same time? } 8... Bxc5 { Good. The bishop recaptures and comes out fighting right away. } (8... Qc7 { You will win it back, but why wander? } 9. b4 { He clings on and gains space. Simpler to recapture at once. }) (8... Be7 { You get c5 back anyway. } 9. O-O { But the bishop stood prouder on c5 itself. }) 9. Bf4 { His bishop eyes c7. Is that anything to fear right now? } 9... O-O { King safe, pieces free to develop, and no locked chain to trap that last bishop — this French never even got the chance to go bad. } (9... Qb6 { Just as strong: b2 and f2 both twitch. } 10. O-O { But b2 is bait with the rook behind it. Castling keeps life simple. }) (9... Nd5 { Poking the bishop. It just steps back. } 10. Bg3 { Nothing gained, and your knight left its post. }) (9... Bd7 { Fine, though his knight lands hard on e5 next. } 10. Ne5 { Castle first, kick later. }) 0-1
